  "markdown_report": "## Insights and directions\n\n**Starting point.** (1) Appended-mask prediction uses preceding events; interior reconstruction can exploit later events. Terminal supervision tests this mismatch. (2) Users report stale-interest recommendations and weak, broadly related matches. Endpoint training may address these failures, but uniform cutoffs do not preferentially sample recent original events. (3) Industrial and Electronics have negative tenth-percentile training margins (−0.2267 and −0.0479). These show ranking weaknesses, without establishing their cause or held-out impact.\n\n**Related work.** (1) [BERT4Rec §3.6](https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06690) describes last-item training examples without specifying their ratio; its released generator does not establish a dedicated terminal-training mixture. (2) [RSS](https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02643) distinguishes uniform from recency-weighted sampling. Neither establishes the best schedule here. (3) [Loss comparisons](https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.07602) motivate fixed objectives and negatives; loss reduction also changes example weighting. (4) [Sampled-metric research](https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/) limits conclusions to the pinned candidate protocol.\n\n## Framework and candidate ideas\n\n| Direction | Intervention | Intended gap |\n|---|---|---|\n| Supervision alignment | Fixed terminal-mask mixture | Interior recovery versus endpoint prediction |\n| Temporal representation | Learned relative-position attention bias | Persistent versus current interests |\n| Ranking supervision | Moderately hard negatives | Weak positive–negative separation |\n\nScores are prospective. O/P/U/M/D denote originality, potential, user alignment, model alignment, and difficulty; higher D means harder. Each cell states a strength and limitation.\n\n| Idea | O | P | U | M | D |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Fixed terminal mixture | 3: controlled adaptation; established principle | 7: useful baseline; uncertain gains | 6: context alignment; no recency guarantee | 6: endpoint separation; cause unproven | 4: sampler change; controls matter |\n| Learned positional bias | 5: flexible relevance; familiar mechanism | 7: retains older signals; overfitting risk | 7: contextual fit; ownership unknown | 5: changes attention; sensitivity unavailable | 6: few parameters; writer changes |\n| Moderate-hard negatives | 4: focused ranking; established sampling | 7: difficult distinctions; false negatives | 5: sharper ranks; relevance uncertain | 8: addresses margins; training evidence | 6: single network; proposal correction |\n\n## Selected experiment: fixed terminal-mask mixture\n\nAt 10% progress, retain the simplest intervention: exactly 50% terminal-prefix examples, otherwise baseline random masking. Uniformly sample eligible training cutoffs, replace targets with the reserved mask, and discard later events. Preserve bidirectional prefix attention.\n\nHypotheses target **older interests treated as continuing demand** and **weak, broadly related matches**, plus weak masked-target separation. Endpoint alignment could help; explicit recency, compatibility, redundancy, and causal preferences remain unestablished.\n\nFirst isolate loss reduction using identical baseline examples/masks. Then compare the mixture against that normalization-matched control, using explicit equal branch coefficients. Preserve loss, negatives, optimizer, architecture, seeds 0/42, four routes, 200 batches/epoch, and maximum 200 epochs. Record target counts and context lengths; one-target/context-matched ablations separate supervision volume and corruption effects when budget permits.\n\nKeep one checkpoint/category, diagnostic APIs, sampler provenance, matching predictor, and final-nonpadding readout. Select by host sampled macro HR@10. Report category hits/200, paired differences and conditional bootstrap intervals when host user-level outcomes exist. Disclose all trials and selection; validation gains remain exploratory.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Test an explicitly balanced mixture of uniform random-prefix terminal-mask examples and baseline random-mask examples within the supplied corrected BERT4Rec. Separate loss-normalization effects from masking effects before attributing any gain. Train exactly one independent model per category and retain appended-mask inference.",
    "motivation": "Terminal examples remove the future context available in interior reconstruction and align supervision with prediction. This may reduce two user-facing failures: treating older interests as continuing demand and ranking weak, broadly related matches above choices supported by the immediate context. These are hypotheses rather than demonstrated remedies: uniform original-position sampling does not explicitly favor recent events, and histories alone cannot establish ownership, compatibility, or incremental usefulness. Industrial_and_Scientific and Electronics show weaker training-exposed margins, motivating a test of endpoint ranking, but their margins do not establish the mechanism or held-out improvement.",
    "implementation_notes": "Start from the supplied corrected BERT4Rec without inspecting other trials or reusing their checkpoints. Preserve the baseline architecture, loss function, negative count and distribution, optimizer, user-sampling distribution, initialization seed, and update count. Define the experiment as endpoint alignment rather than recency weighting.\n\nNormalization control: determine the supplied baseline's actual reduction from its provided code. If it averages all supervised tokens together, first compare that baseline with per-example averaging using identical sampled histories, corrupted inputs, targets, negatives, and update schedule. These are separate single-model trials, never fused. The primary masking comparison must use the same per-example reduction in control and treatment. For the treatment, construct equal-sized terminal and random branches and use loss = 0.5 * mean(terminal example losses) + 0.5 * mean(random example losses). Each example loss is the mean over its valid supervised positions. With an odd batch size, use a declared seeded alternating allocation and separate branch means. Equal coefficients do not imply equal gradient norms. Do not silently change normalization and attribute the entire difference to terminal masking.\n\nFor terminal examples, sample a cutoff j uniformly from 1 through len(history)-1 using zero-based indexing, requiring at least one preceding interaction. The target is history[j]; retain history[max(0,j-(L-1)):j], append the reserved MASK, and left-pad to L. Never include the target event or any later event. Earlier occurrences of the same item remain valid historical context. Random-mask examples retain corrected baseline corruption with supervised targets hidden; ensure every example has at least one valid supervised target. Use the same eligible user distribution in the normalization-matched comparison, explicitly reporting any exclusions required by histories shorter than two events. Continue the baseline negative sampler and its existing exclusions unchanged; document their exact behavior rather than silently introducing full-history exclusions or a different proposal.\n\nThe primary mixture also changes available context and supervised-target count, so it cannot isolate target placement alone. Record branch example counts, supervised-target counts, original cutoffs, retained/visible context lengths, corruption counts, and optional sampled branch gradient norms. Record provenance separately without changing the loader's (None, sequence, positive, negative) contract. If budget permits, a separate one-target control compares terminal-only, exactly-one-random-mask, and their mixture using matched user and pre-corruption sequence-length distributions; future-context availability remains an intentional treatment difference. Further controls can hold corrupted inputs fixed and supervise either all masked positions or one uniformly selected mask: with per-example normalization their expected gradients agree, but variance differs. A separate one-mask-versus-multiple-mask comparison with one supervised target measures corruption effects. Target-budget matching is an additional experiment, not interchangeable with the required fixed-update comparison. Defer recency-weighted endpoints, positional biases, and negative-hardness changes until endpoint alignment is assessed.\n\nKeep preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, exactly 200 batches per epoch, and at most 200 epochs. Retain Movies_and_TV, Industrial_and_Scientific, Electronics, and CDs_and_Vinyl routing; DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR']; save artifacts beneath the trial directory. Execute single-GPU within the shared 43,200 GPU-second search budget. Predeclare the 50% mixture, comparisons, and unchanged host-compatible checkpoint-selection rule before observing their validation outcomes. Each trial trains one model per category; prediction loads exactly one selected checkpoint per category, without averaging, independently fitted auxiliary scores, or fusion.\n\nPreserve ModelView, log2feats(sequence), item_emb, predict_score(features, items), DiagnosisProbe(view, device).run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Retain the four-tuple diagnostic loader and return measured metrics with definitions. Distinguish terminal and interior training margins when provenance is available; otherwise mark that distinction unavailable. Training-exposed margins and omitted history-sensitivity results cannot establish held-out gains or a preferred recency direction. Embedding alignment near 0.5 alone does not establish collapse or representation quality. Sampler provenance, fresh-process predictor parity, and attention-deletion checks remain unavailable until genuinely implemented and measured. Preserve existing diagnostic fixtures and add provenance or parity checks only through supported interfaces.\n\nThe embedded predictor writer must reproduce the trained architecture, ID mapping, category routing, reserved token IDs, and appended-mask construction. Truncate history to L-1 before appending MASK. Find the last nonpadding position from actual indices, never count-1. A fresh-process reload and score comparison is needed before claiming checkpoint/predictor parity; live-model checks alone are insufficient. Restrict information to public histories, titles, descriptions, and timestamps. Do not use unavailable ratings, review text, item categories/prices/stores, simulator traits, held-out target timestamps, or test files.\n\nLabel the host metric sampled HR@10: one positive plus 99 unique negatives, 200 validation users/category, evaluation seed 42. Report each category's hits/200 and HR@10, its difference from baseline, and the equally weighted macro difference. One extra hit changes category HR by 0.5 percentage points and macro HR by 0.125 points. If the host supplies paired user-level outcomes, compute a category-stratified paired bootstrap with 10,000 resamples and analysis seed 42, resampling 200 user pairs within each category while retaining fixed candidate outcomes; report the percentile 95% interval for the macro difference. If those outcomes are unavailable, report the interval as unavailable rather than reconstructing or inventing it. Intervals condition on fitted models, categories, and candidates and exclude training-seed variability, candidate-sampling variability, and adaptive selection. Disclose all trials, checkpoint evaluations, budget use, baseline/selected configurations, and selection rule. Treat validation findings as exploratory and freeze selection before the host's untouched final evaluation. Do not interpret sampled gains as full-catalog gains or infer generalization across seeds or domains.",
    "pseudocode": "Set preprocessing_seed=0 and training_seed=42.\nDeclare mixture=0.5, fixed hyperparameters, comparisons, and checkpoint rule.\nUse only supplied training histories and unchanged host validation.\n\nNormalization control, if baseline uses pooled token averaging:\n    Compare baseline reduction versus per-example reduction.\n    Reuse identical sampled examples, masks, negatives, and update schedule.\n    Train separate models; never combine their predictions.\n\nPrimary comparison: normalized random-mask control versus normalized mixture.\nFor each required category:\n    Initialize one supplied corrected BERT4Rec with seed 42.\n    For each epoch, at most 200:\n        For each of exactly 200 batches:\n            Sample eligible users with the declared baseline-compatible distribution.\n            For mixture treatment, assign exactly half to each branch.\n            For normalized control, use baseline random masking throughout.\n            For each terminal example with history h:\n                j = seeded_uniform_integer(1, len(h)-1)  # inclusive\n                prefix = h[max(0, j-(L-1)):j]\n                sequence = left_pad(prefix + [MASK], L)\n                positive = zeros(L); positive[L-1] = h[j]\n                negative = unchanged_baseline_negative_sampler(h, target=h[j])\n                Record cutoff, original target position, and corruption provenance.\n            For each random example:\n                sequence, positive, negative = corrected_baseline_masking(h)\n                Require at least one supervised reserved-mask position.\n            Compute unchanged unreduced loss at valid supervised positions.\n            example_loss = mean(valid position losses within each example)\n            If treatment:\n                loss = 0.5*mean(terminal example_loss)\n                     + 0.5*mean(random example_loss)\n            Else:\n                loss = mean(example_loss)\n            Update the single network using the unchanged optimizer.\n            Record branch counts, target counts, and context-length summaries.\n    Select and save one checkpoint using the declared host-compatible rule.\nWrite the matching category-routed predictor and model configuration.\n\nFor prediction:\n    Load exactly one checkpoint for the requested category.\n    sequence = left_pad(history[-(L-1):] + [MASK], L)\n    features = model.log2feats(sequence)\n    index = max(position where sequence[position] != PAD)\n    scores = model.predict_score(features[index], candidate_items)\nVerify reload parity through a fresh-process runner if available.\n\nObtain sampled HR@10 only from the unchanged host evaluator.\nReport category hits, category differences, and macro difference.\nIf paired user outcomes are supplied:\n    Use 10,000 seed-42 category-stratified paired bootstrap resamples.\n    Report percentile 95% interval with conditional-uncertainty limitations.\nDisclose every evaluated trial/checkpoint and freeze final selection.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 3,
      "positive": "Provides a controlled, diagnosis-motivated experiment separating endpoint supervision, example weighting, and supervision volume within corrected BERT4Rec.",
      "negative": "Last-item supervision is already described in BERT4Rec; neither the 50% mixture nor the normalization controls constitute a new backbone or established algorithmic advance."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "A clean result could guide subsequent temporal-representation and ranking-supervision experiments while making their comparisons easier to interpret.",
      "negative": "The mixture remains unvalidated, uniform endpoints do not establish a recency benefit, and the approach cannot directly resolve compatibility, redundant functions, or unsupported ownership assumptions."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "The primary intervention preserves the architecture and inference contract and mainly changes sampling and explicit loss reduction.",
      "negative": "Reproducible normalization controls, exact branch accounting, provenance, diagnostic adaptation, and fresh-process predictor verification add work; optional ablations also consume the shared training budget."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
