      "title": "Official BERT4Rec training-data generator",
      "link": "https://github.com/FeiSun/BERT4Rec/blob/master/gen_data.py",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides implementation context for random-mask duplication and the explicit force_last path."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The cited force_last=True invocation is for evaluation; this file does not establish a dedicated terminal-only training mixture.",
        "Paper-level descriptions and released implementation behavior should be distinguished."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Petrov and Macdonald (2022), recency-sampling research",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02643",
      "contributions": [
        "Motivates task-aligned sampling and distinguishes uniform target selection from recency-weighted selection.",
        "Uses sampling weights proportional to alpha^(n-i); alpha below one favors later original positions."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "RSS removes sampled targets and is not identical to prefix-terminal masking.",
        "Does not establish gains or an optimal schedule for the proposed BERT4Rec mixture on this benchmark."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Klenitskiy and Vasilev (2023), sequential-recommendation loss comparison",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.07602",
      "contributions": [
        "Highlights the importance of loss and negative sampling in sequential-recommendation comparisons."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not validate the proposed terminal-mask mixture.",
        "Supports controlling these factors rather than changing them simultaneously."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "PyTorch CrossEntropyLoss documentation",
      "link": "https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.CrossEntropyLoss.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Documents unreduced loss and mean reduction over nonignored targets.",
        "Supports implementing explicit per-example and per-branch coefficients."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The supplied baseline's actual loss and reduction must be checked rather than assumed.",
        "Equal loss coefficients do not imply equal gradient norms or optimization influence."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Krichene and Rendle, On Sampled Metrics for Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/",
      "contributions": [
        "Shows that sampled recommendation metrics can differ from full-catalog metrics and can reverse model rankings."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not authorize changing the pinned evaluator.",
        "Fixed candidates improve reproducibility but do not eliminate sampling bias."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Cañamares et al. (2020), user-level statistical comparisons in recommendation",
      "link": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10791-020-09371-3",
      "contributions": [
        "Supports user-level statistical comparison of recommendation outcomes.",
        "Motivates preserving user pairing when comparing baseline and candidate results."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The proposed category-stratified paired bootstrap is an analysis choice requiring host-provided paired outcomes.",
        "Its interval does not capture training-seed, candidate-sampling, or adaptive-selection uncertainty."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Cawley and Talbot, On Over-fitting in Model Selection and Subsequent Selection Bias in Performance Evaluation",
      "link": "https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Explains how repeated model selection can overfit evaluation criteria and create optimistic performance estimates."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "An ordinary bootstrap around the selected validation winner does not correct adaptive-search optimism.",
        "Confirmatory conclusions require the host's untouched final evaluation after selection is frozen."
      ]
    }
  ]
